# Typography

## Family rules (hard)

- **Newsreader (serif)** — long-form body copy only. Paragraphs inside
  `<article>`, blockquotes, page descriptions.
- **JetBrains Mono** — **all UI chrome**: wordmark, nav, post titles,
  datelines, sidebar, archive list rows, rate prices, the CTA, footer,
  section headings (`.section-h`).

Yes, post **titles** are mono. That's the whole personality of this design;
do not change them to serif "to be more readable." They render at
`var(--fs-title)` (18px) `semi-bold` and read fine.

## Settings

```css
body {
  font-family: var(--font-serif);
  font-size: var(--fs-body);
  line-height: var(--lh-body);
  font-feature-settings: 'kern' 1, 'liga' 1;
  -webkit-font-smoothing: antialiased;
  text-rendering: optimizeLegibility;
}
```

Mono elements should set `letter-spacing: -0.01em` on titles & wordmark,
`0` elsewhere. Uppercase mono labels (datelines, eyebrows) use no extra
letter-spacing — JetBrains Mono is already comfortably tracked.

## Hierarchy

```
h1 (page title)   var(--fs-h1)     mono semi-bold       22/1.35
h2 (post title)   var(--fs-title)  mono semi-bold       18/1.3
h2 (section)      14px             mono semi-bold caps  + 0.04em tracking
year heading      13px             mono semi-bold       (accent color)
dateline          11px             mono uppercase       (meta color)
body              17px             serif                17/1.6
sidebar           12px             mono                 line-height inherit
```

No `<h3>` and below in the current spec. If new content needs deeper
hierarchy, add a new token first.

## Don'ts

- No web font swap flash — preconnect to `fonts.gstatic.com` and use
  `display=swap`. The page should fall back to Georgia / monospace
  cleanly.
- No `text-transform: uppercase` on body copy. Mono labels only.
- No font sizes outside the token scale.
- No italic everywhere — italic is reserved for `<em>` inside body
  (per-word emphasis) and for quoted titles (e.g. "*The Notes* podcast").
